.TH ARME 1 "2026-08-01" "scheduler" "Zach's utilities"
.SH NAME
arme \- arm a recurring job that never spends
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B arme list
.br
.B arme jobs
.br
.B arme check
.br
.B arme plan
.br
.\" bashify: norun
.B arme apply
.br
.B arme
[\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-\-version\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B arme
arms the recurring maintenance this ecosystem runs for free \- work with no
model in the loop and nothing to meter. It is the companion of
.BR dose ,
which apportions the metered kind.
.PP
.B Why the two are separate verbs.
Every job kind
.B dose
emits \- RUNNER, SWEEP, BATCH \- is metered work or the dispatch of it, and
every one is gated by
.IR USAGE_CEILING .
A monitor wired through any of them inherits that gate, so it stops reporting
exactly when the quota is exhausted:
.PP
.RS
"quota exhausted" and "nothing to report" become one silence.
.RE
.PP
Those are two world\-states sharing a symbol, which is the collapse this
family of tools exists to catch \- here at the scheduling layer rather than
inside a sensor. A flag on an existing kind does not fix it, because the gate
lives in the runner those kinds are dispatched by. A separate verb, a separate
register and a separate block do.
.SS The register
.I schedule/_monitor.conf
holds one line per job:
.I name|enabled|cron|command|attest
in the same shape
.I _paced.conf
already uses. A
.I schedule/_monitor.<host>.conf
entry of the same name replaces the shared one, on the shared\-then\-host rule
.I _runner.conf
and
.I _sweep.conf
follow.
.SS How "never spends" is established
It is established or refused, never assumed.
.B check
reads every entry and reports one verdict per line, from a closed set:
.TP
.B PARKED
declared with enabled=0. It stays visible rather than vanishing, because an
entry nobody can see is one nobody reconsiders.
.TP
.B BADCRON
the schedule is not five fields, so it never fires.
.TP
.B NOTFOUND
the command is not executable.
.TP
.B SPENDS
the command names a model runner. Refused outright.
.TP
.B FREE
an obvious leaf: readable, naming no model runner, handing control to nothing.
.TP
.B UNVERIFIED
anything else, including a command that hands off to another program.
.PP
Only
.BR PARKED ,
.B FREE
and an attested
.B UNVERIFIED
permit arming; every other verdict refuses.
.PP
.B UNVERIFIED is not a pass.
Static reading cannot settle this question, and behaving as though it could is
the defect this section exists because of: two jobs that do spend passed an
earlier version of this check, because one hands off to a script that resolves
its engine at run time and names nothing a reader can follow.
.PP
An UNVERIFIED entry is cleared by the fifth field, an
.BR attestation :
a sentence stating why the job cannot spend. The gate then holds a written
claim, kept in version control, instead of a guess. A command that names a
model runner is refused whatever its attestation says.
.SS The crontab
.B apply
writes its own block, delimited by its own markers, and copies every other
line through untouched \- including the
.I scheduler\-managed
block.
.I sync\-crontab.sh
extends the same courtesy in reverse, reading a table before rewriting it so
unmanaged lines are never discarded. Two blocks, two owners, neither able to
eat the other's work.
.PP
This verb writes the crontab where
.BR transplante (1)
refuses to. That is not an inconsistency: the crontab belongs to
.BR scheduler ,
and this verb ships inside it. A tool may write what it owns.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Not wired for any subcommand of this verb. Requesting it is reported rather
than ignored.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress commentary; results only.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and the exit vocabulary.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The promise was kept.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error; the caller is wrong.
.TP
.B 4
GAP: there is no register to read, so nothing is armed and nothing declares
that anything should be.
.TP
.B 5
BROKEN: it ran with valid arguments and failed to finish.
.TP
.B 7
REFUSED: at least one entry cannot be armed as declared \- a bad schedule, a
command that is missing, one that names a model runner, or one whose freedom
from cost is unverified and unattested.
.SH EXAMPLES
Listing prints this verb's own subcommands, bare, one per line, so a caller
reads the surface without a parser:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ arme
list
jobs
check
plan
apply
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B jobs
prints the armed monitors the same way \- the register's surface rather than
the verb's:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ arme jobs
ecosim-sensors
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B plan
shows the block
.B apply
would install, so it can be read before it is installed:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ arme plan
# >>> arme-managed (non-agent monitors -- edit schedule/_monitor.conf, then `arme apply`) >>>
*/30 * * * * /home/zach/.local/bin/ecosim-sensor-tick # arme:ecosim-sensors:MONITOR (non-agent, never usage-gated)
# <<< arme-managed <<<
.fi
.RE
.PP
An unknown subcommand is the caller's error:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ arme frobnicate
arme: unknown subcommand: frobnicate  (try: arme list)
$ echo $?
2
.fi
.RE
.SH COST
Nothing here spends money, and neither does anything it arms \- that is the
whole point of the verb. It offers no
.B \-\-summon
flag for any subcommand.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I schedule/_monitor.conf
The register of jobs that run for free.
.TP
.I bin/arme
This utility.
.TP
.I lib/verb.sh
The shared runtime it sources.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dose (1),
.BR crontab (5),
.BR crontab (1),
.BR transplante (1)
.PP
Expectations transfer from
.BR crontab (5)
for the schedule format, and from
.BR dose (1)
for everything this verb deliberately is not.
.SH HISTORY
Coined 2026-08-01 with
.B bashify coin
onto a branch that already carried
.BR dose ,
under the rule that one noun carries many verbs. It exists because the
scheduling vocabulary had three job kinds and all three were metered.
